---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Demo from '../components/Demo.astro'
import { url } from '../lib/url'
---

<Base title="Demo — jukebox" description="The jukebox interface, running in the page on a fabricated library.">
  <section class="wrap wide" style="padding-top:44px">
    <h1 style="margin:0 0 10px">The interface</h1>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:70ch">
      The actual front end from <code>apps/web</code>, built in demo mode and framed below — not a screenshot. Same
      components, same virtualized table, same keyboard handling; the library it browses is fabricated in the browser
      instead of coming from a server.
    </p>
    <Demo note="A fabricated library, generated in the browser — no server behind it. Four themes, switchable from the status bar." />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap prose" style="margin-top:60px">
    <h2>What to look at</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The table</b> — TanStack v9, virtualized: the row count is not what makes it slow, the network is.</li>
      <li>
        <b>The four themes</b> — iTunes 8, iTunes 12, Apple Music, and <b>Studio</b>: dark canvas, floating panels,
        one green. Not palettes — row height, artwork, navigation and density all change with the skin.
      </li>
      <li><b>Bulk editing</b> — select several tracks, edit once; the modal adapts to what the selection has in common.</li>
      <li><b>The column browser</b> — genre, artist, album, the way iTunes did it.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="note">
      In the real application every one of these views is a client of the public API, exactly like a third-party
      front end would be. See <a href={url('/docs/api/')}>the API reference</a>.
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
